+"""Native S-unit equations over finite modules for arXiv:2505.19141.
+
+The paper studies equations in which Laurent monomials act on elements of a
+finitely presented module.  Here the module is M = F_p^k + F_p^k and the one
+Laurent generator X acts by a square-zero shear
+
+    X(a, b) = (a + U b, b).
+
+For the standard bottom-basis vectors m_i, the equation
+sum_i X**z_i m_i = (t, 1) is exactly U z = t modulo p.  The generator samples
+z first and constructs t, so it never solves a generated instance.  U is a
+dense wrapped-geometric circulant.  Generic modular Gaussian elimination is
+the honest Track-B reference algorithm; recognizing the circulant invariant
+turns its inverse into one cyclic first difference.
+"""
+
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import hashlib
+import itertools
+import json
+import math
+import os
+import random
+import re
+import time
+
+
+TRACK: str = "B"
+
+PROBLEM_PROFILE: dict = {
+    "native_domain": "algebra",
+    "object_regime": "finite_field",
+    "computational_core": "linear_algebra",
+    "certificate_form": "integer_tuple",
+    "native_objects": [
+        "finite-dimensional module over F_p[X^{+1},X^{-1}]",
+        "invertible matrix action of the Laurent generator X",
+        "module vectors in F_p^k direct-sum F_p^k",
+        "integer exponent tuple",
+    ],
+    "verification_operations": [
+        "exact modular monomial-action substitution",
+        "exact finite-field vector addition",
+        "coordinatewise equality in the presented module",
+    ],
+    "domain_essentiality": "native",
+    "reduction_kind": "none",
+    "reduction": None,
+    "reduction_source": "paper_central",
+    "intuition_type": "invariant",
+    "intuition_description": (
+        "A dense circulant action has a wrapped-geometric kernel, so one cyclic "
+        "first difference inverts it; without recognizing this invariant a solver "
+        "faces a dense modular linear system."
+    ),
+    "hardness_basis": (
+        "Track B: Gauss-Jordan elimination over F_p solves the promised family in "
+        "O(k^3) field operations; at the hard preset (k=64, p=1000003) the exact "
+        "reference implementation measured 17689 field operations and 0.0141 "
+        "seconds mean over eight shipping seeds, while the wrapped-geometric "
+        "first-difference route uses at most 266 field operations and must be "
+        "recognized and executed without tools."
+    ),
+    "max_answer_tokens": 112,
+}
+
+NATIVE: dict = {
+    "domain": PROBLEM_PROFILE["native_domain"],
+    "core": PROBLEM_PROFILE["computational_core"],
+    "objects": PROBLEM_PROFILE["native_objects"],
+    "intuition": (
+        PROBLEM_PROFILE["intuition_type"]
+        + ": "
+        + PROBLEM_PROFILE["intuition_description"]
+    ),
+    "reduction": PROBLEM_PROFILE["reduction"],
+}
+
+CERTIFICATE_LANGUAGE: dict = {
+    "description": (
+        "A list of exactly k canonical exponent residues z_0,...,z_{k-1}; "
+        "each entry is an integer in [0,p-1].  The module action has period p, "
+        "so these residues represent all integer exponent classes."
+    ),
+    "bounds": {
+        "max_exponents": 256,
+        "min_residue": 0,
+        "max_modulus": 2_147_483_647,
+        "max_atomic_elements": 256,
+    },
+}
+
+DIFFICULTY: dict = {
+    "demo": {"n": 3, "modulus": 11},
+    "easy": {"n": 12, "modulus": 101},
+    "medium": {"n": 32, "modulus": 10_007},
+    "hard": {"n": 64, "modulus": 1_000_003},
+}
+SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY = "hard"
+
+STRUCTURAL_HINT: str = (
+    "Hint: The dense action block is circulant, and its cyclic kernel is a wrapped geometric progression."
+)
+PLACEBO_HINT: str = (
+    "Hint: The dense action table rewards careful attention to the modular arithmetic and coordinate indexing."
+)
+
+# Populated after the three transcript-producing harden.py runs.  The diagnostic
+# is deliberately separate from the only remaining G9 gate, the size/effort cap.
+_G9_EVIDENCE = None
+
+NOTES = r"""
+Paper definition. Section 1, Equation (1.2), defines an S-unit equation as a
+sum of Laurent monomials acting on elements of a finitely presented module.
+Section 3.3, especially Proposition 3.5 and the paragraph following its proof,
+puts the action on a free finite-rank module into commuting invertible matrices.
+This generator uses exactly that matrix-action object with one Laurent
+generator; it does not replace the module by a graph, SAT instance, or other
+surrogate.
+
+What produces a certificate and what it costs. Theorem 1.3 proves that over a
+prime-power torsion module the entire exponent solution set is effectively
+p-normal. Section 3.5 constructs the accepting automaton. Thus a Track-A claim
+would be false in this regime. In this promised finite-dimensional subfamily,
+direct substitution reduces recovery further to a dense k by k modular linear
+system, and exact Gauss-Jordan elimination solves it in O(k^3). At k=64 the
+implementation measured 17689 field operations and 0.0141 seconds mean over
+eight shipping seeds. The compact route recognizes that the first column is one wrapped
+geometric progression: its unique failed ratio identifies the wrap, and one
+cyclic first difference gives all 64 exponents in at most 266 field operations.
+This is therefore Track B.
+
+Construction. Sample the exponent tuple z uniformly first. Choose a nonzero
+ratio a with a^k != 1, and let q_d=(1-a^k)^(-1) a^((d+s) mod k). Set
+U[r,c]=q_((r-c) mod k), then t=Uz. The module is M=F_p^k direct-sum F_p^k with
+X(a,b)=(a+Ub,b). Since (X-I)^2=0, X^z(0,e_i)=(z Ue_i,e_i), so the sampled tuple
+is a certificate by composition of identities. make_instance never calls
+Gaussian elimination or the compact inverse.
+
+Easy regimes and attacks. Theorem 1.3 makes every prime-power instance
+decidable; Corollary 1.5 also gives decidability for torsion with at most two
+prime divisors, while Theorem 1.4 ties three or more primes to the open general
+linear-exponential problem. This family intentionally stays in the effective
+prime regime and labels the algorithm. Uniform z and invertible U make t
+uniform, eliminating a planted-value outlier. The adversary panel checks a
+diagonal outlier guess, one-pass coordinate greed, 256 uniform restarts, and
+the in-context ansatz z=t. The successful dense elimination is reported only
+as the Track-B reference algorithm.
+""".strip()
