+NOTES = r"""
+Paper definition. Section 1, Equation (1.2), defines an S-unit equation as a
+sum of Laurent monomials acting on elements of a finitely presented module.
+Section 3.3, especially Proposition 3.5 and the paragraph following its proof,
+puts the action on a free finite-rank module into commuting invertible matrices.
+This generator uses exactly that matrix-action object with one Laurent
+generator; it does not replace the module by a graph, SAT instance, or other
+surrogate.
+
+What produces a certificate and what it costs. Theorem 1.3 proves that over a
+prime-power torsion module the entire exponent solution set is effectively
+p-normal. Section 3.5 constructs the accepting automaton. Thus a Track-A claim
+would be false in this regime. In this promised finite-dimensional subfamily,
+direct substitution reduces recovery further to a dense k by k modular linear
+system, and exact Gauss-Jordan elimination solves it in O(k^3). At k=64 the
+implementation measured 17689 field operations and 0.0141 seconds mean over
+eight shipping seeds. The compact route recognizes that the first column is one wrapped
+geometric progression: its unique failed ratio identifies the wrap, and one
+cyclic first difference gives all 64 exponents in at most 266 field operations.
+This is therefore Track B.
+
+Construction. Sample the exponent tuple z uniformly first. Choose a nonzero
+ratio a with a^k != 1, and let q_d=(1-a^k)^(-1) a^((d+s) mod k). Set
+U[r,c]=q_((r-c) mod k), then t=Uz. The module is M=F_p^k direct-sum F_p^k with
+X(a,b)=(a+Ub,b). Since (X-I)^2=0, X^z(0,e_i)=(z Ue_i,e_i), so the sampled tuple
+is a certificate by composition of identities. make_instance never calls
+Gaussian elimination or the compact inverse.
+
+Easy regimes and attacks. Theorem 1.3 makes every prime-power instance
+decidable; Corollary 1.5 also gives decidability for torsion with at most two
+prime divisors, while Theorem 1.4 ties three or more primes to the open general
+linear-exponential problem. This family intentionally stays in the effective
+prime regime and labels the algorithm. Uniform z and invertible U make t
+uniform, eliminating a planted-value outlier. The adversary panel checks a
+diagonal outlier guess, one-pass coordinate greed, 256 uniform restarts, and
+the in-context ansatz z=t. The successful dense elimination is reported only
+as the Track-B reference algorithm.
+""".strip()
