 Equation (1.2) in Section 1 fixes the native S-unit definition, and Proposition 3.16 in Section 3.3 represents the Laurent generators as commuting invertible matrices on a free module. Theorem 1.3 and Section 3.5 give an effective automaton—and ultimately a `p`-normal solution set—for prime-power torsion. Corollary 1.5 also gives decidability with at most two torsion primes. Those results rule out claiming Track A here.
 
-For this promised subfamily, generic exact Gauss-Jordan elimination solves `Uz=t` in `O(k^3)`. Across eight hard instances it used a mean 30,219 field operations (maximum 97,822) and 0.002143 seconds mean in the recorded final self-test. The shorter route notices that the first column of `U` is a geometric sequence with one cyclic wrap and a ratio promised to lie in `{2,...,9}`. Three adjacent entries identify that small ratio without modular division. If the wrap is at `d`, then `z[r-d] = t[r+1] - a*t[r] (mod p)`. Detecting the ratio and applying that difference costs 280 field operations at shipping size. The benchmark asks a no-tool solver to find and execute that compression; it does not claim computational intractability.
